  "description": "full planning application in respect of the above property for the internal reconfigura-tion of the approved loft space to facilitate the creation of one additional studio room with en-suite facilities, thereby increasing the property from a six-bedroom HMO (Use Class C4) to a seven-room, eight-person Large HMO (Sui Generis).\nThe property benefits from an established and lawful HMO use pursuant to planning permission reference 18/01925/FUL, together with subsequent minor amendment approval (18/01925/FULMIN). Furthermore, planning permission was granted under reference 25/02506/FUL for a single storey rear extension and roof space conversion including rear dormer. The current proposal does not seek to enlarge or alter the ap-proved external envelope, but instead utilises the previously consented loft accom-modation more efficiently through internal reconfiguration only.\nThe proposal retains the generous 32m² communal kitchen, dining and living area at ground floor level, which significantly exceeds typical communal space expectations for the intended occupancy level. All bedrooms meet or exceed nationally prescribed minimum HMO space standards as well as London Borough of Bexley local planning standards. The new studio room is provided with its own en-suite and kitchenette fa-cilities. Fire safety upgrades, compliant means of escape, and appropriate internal compartmentation will be secured through Building Regulations and HMO licensing requirements.\nSecure cycle storage is provided within the existing 9.11m² garage structure, capable of accommodating the required number of long-stay cycle spaces in accordance with the London Plan. Adequate refuse and recycling storage is also available within the curtilage, consistent with local waste management guidance.\nA comprehensive Planning Statement accompanies this submission, assessing the proposal against the National Planning Policy Framework, the London Plan (2021), and the London Borough of Bexley Local Plan. The development has been demonstrated to represent sustainable development, making efficient use of previously approved floorspace, supporting housing choice, and maintaining the character and amenity of the surrounding residential area.\n",
